WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.091
Errors are results, not exceptions.

2
00:00:02.371 --> 00:00:03.395
Tools fail.

3
00:00:03.675 --> 00:00:09.925
The person isn’t in the directory, the date phrase is “sometime soon,” the downstream API times out.

4
00:00:10.205 --> 00:00:16.029
The wrong reaction is to let the exception kill your loop, or to swallow it and return an empty string.

5
00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:22.859
The right reaction is to tell the model, in a tool_result with is_error set to true and a message it can act on.

6
00:00:23.139 --> 00:00:25.165
Anthropic’s docs give two reasons.

7
00:00:25.445 --> 00:00:29.712
The model can incorporate the failure into its answer instead of inventing one.

8
00:00:29.992 --> 00:00:38.184
And with an instructive message, it can often recover: the docs suggest saying what went wrong and what to try next, rather than a bare “failed.

9
00:00:38.464 --> 00:00:40.235
” “No teammate matches Jordan.

10
00:00:40.515 --> 00:00:44.803
Known teammates: Priya Raman, Marcus Lee…” gives the model a path forward.

11
00:00:45.083 --> 00:00:54.981
The docs also note that when Claude sends a call with missing parameters and you report it as an error, it typically retries a few times with corrections before giving up.

12
00:00:55.261 --> 00:01:04.477
With strict mode on, malformed calls of that kind should not reach you at all, but your own semantic checks still produce errors, and they follow the same rule.

13
00:01:04.757 --> 00:01:11.797
This is also your first checks-and-balances habit for tools: every call and its error status is a line you can log.

14
00:01:12.077 --> 00:01:18.605
When you build evals in Studio 3, “how often does the model call a tool with input that my validator rejects?

15
00:01:18.885 --> 00:01:21.360
” is one of the most useful numbers you can have.
